WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
A distant exoplanet hosts winds racing at 5,400 miles per hour, far faster than anything in our solar system. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 191. Researchers measured atmospheric winds moving at 5,400 miles per hour on a planet outside the solar system. The observations used spectroscopy to track cloud movements across the exoplanet surface. These speeds exceed any recorded wind in our solar system.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:43.340
High resolution spectra captured the Doppler shift of cloud features as they circled the planet. The extreme circulation pattern suggests powerful global dynamics driven by intense stellar heating.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:51.660
Satellite emissions from starlink reach up to 10,000 times stronger than the signals the square kilometer array low needs to detect.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:01:03.600
The interference appears in protected frequency bands reserved for cosmic studies. Ground-based arrays face growing challenges separating satellite emissions from faint astronomical sources.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:15.820
Observers must develop new filtering techniques to isolate genuine signals amid the rising background. Hardware modifications on future satellites or adjustments to orbital planes could reduce the impact.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:39.260
JWST detected water vapor in the inner zone of a proto-planetary disk where rocky planets form. The vapor sits inside the snow line where temperatures normally keep water as gas. Ultraviolet radiation from the central star liberates water molecules from incoming icy pebbles. The reservoir may supply the building blocks for atmospheres on forming rocky planets.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:53.040
The detected column density matches levels seen in much colder outer disks. Venus likely never formed a moon according to the new analysis. Any hypothetical satellite would have been torn apart quickly by the planet gravity.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:02:05.180
The study concludes the title forces near Venus make long-term moon retention impossible. Prominent planetary astrophysicist modeling shows orbital decay would occur on short-time scales.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:17.740
Close-in moons around Venus face gravitational stresses far stronger than those experienced by Earth satellite. Population three stars formed from pristine hydrogen and helium with no heavier elements.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:29.920
A new paper examines how massive these first starbursts could grow before feedback halted further collapse. JWST observations may soon test the predicted upper mass boundary.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:36.660
The analysis explores the role of radiation pressure and supernova feedback in limiting total stellar mass.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:43.320
JWST detected an over-density of galaxies clearing a region where light could travel freely.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:53.060
Neutral hydrogen previously blocked photons across the early universe. The bubble shows how clustered star-forming regions drove cosmic reionization.

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:59.860
Light from these galaxies ionized surrounding gas and created transparent pockets amid the neutral medium.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:09.800
September new moon creates dark skies ideal for spotting the faint cone of interplanetary dust. The glow rises above the eastern horizon before dawn.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:20.020
Observers can photograph the feature during this limited window. The interplanetary dust scatters sunlight into a diffuse pyramid shape visible under moonless conditions.

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:26.400
This week offers one of the best annual opportunities for northern hemisphere viewers to capture the phenomenon.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:37.720
Type 1A supernova SN 2026 AAIV was discovered in the galaxy NGC 7331 at 45 million light years.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:46.900
Amateur astronomers can still observe and image the event before it fades. The explosion offers a rare chance to study a standard candle in real time.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:59.580
NGC 7331 presents a face-on spiral similar in size and structure to the Milky Way. The supernova peak brightness provides a calibration point for distance measurements across cosmic scales.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:08.560
Laboratory work has reproduced conditions deep inside Neptune and Uranus. A new form of ice was found that holds up at those pressures and temperatures.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:17.920
This discovery aids in understanding the makeup of these ice giants. Experiments use diamond anvil cells with laser heating to mimic the mantles.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:24.580
Predictions about how heat moves and how magnetic fields work inside them will change because of this phase.

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:37.580
IIZW096 shows two galaxies colliding at a high rate of star formation. Chandra X-ray data reveal powerful black hole activity and hot gas in magenta.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:47.980
Optical and infrared views complete the multi-wave length portrait of the system. The merger drives intense bursts of star formation while feeding the central black holes.

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:55.140
Combined data sets highlight how galaxy interactions reshape both stellar populations and nuclear activity.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:00.940
Early solar system models suggest the sun could have consumed a super earth-sized body.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:06.280
Remaining chemical signatures in the sun's composition may preserve evidence of that event.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:11.660
The hypothesis links planetary migration to the star's final inventory of elements.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:18.140
Refractory elements depleted in the solar photosphere align with predictions for a swallowed rocky planet.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:24.320
Migration simulations show how an inner super earth could have been driven into the star during the disk phase.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:31.240
NASA and IBM released an open source model trained on 17 years of lunar reconnaissance orbiter data.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:37.900
The NASA IBM lunar foundation model identifies ice deposits, craters, and volcanic features.

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:42.740
Code and weights are available on hugging face and GitHub for public use.

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:48.320
The model processes multispectral imagery to map surface composition at high resolution.

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:54.780
Researchers can now apply the same architecture to other planetary data sets for rapid feature detection.

00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:01.540
Volunteers can inspect images from Euclid and the Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope to flag artifacts.

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:07.780
The Citizen Science Project improves data quality for distant galaxy and exoplanet studies.

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:13.100
Participants learn how raw telescope frames are cleaned before scientific analysis.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:21.120
The platform presents calibrated exposures and asks users to mark cosmic rays, satellite trails, and optical ghosts.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:27.060
Refined catalogs will support precise measurements of galaxy shapes and weak lensing signals.

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:33.120
The four crew 12 astronauts will hold a news conference from the space station on September 16.

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:38.080
They will recap mission highlights and answer questions before their return to Earth.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:40.600
NASA will stream the event live.

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:47.180
The crew includes NASA astronauts Jessica Mir and Jack Hathaway, along with international partners.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:52.640
The session offers the final public update before the capsule departs the orbiting laboratory.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:59.040
The 1966 mission practiced rendezvous and docking techniques needed for Apollo lunar flights.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:04.840
Crew performed the first direct descent rendezvous and a tethered gravity gradient experiment.

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:09.020
The flight remains a key milestone in human space flight development.

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:16.420
Gemini 11 demonstrated precision orbital maneuvers that later enabled lunar orbit insertion and module docking.

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:22.020
The tethered satellite test provided early data on gravity gradient stabilization in orbit.

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:27.700
Next time keep an eye on the crew 12 astronauts news conference scheduled for September 16.

